130	  - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed.
131	  - Run a git log command to see recent commit messages, so that you can follow this repository's commit message style.
132	2. Analyze all staged changes (both previously staged and newly added) and draft a commit message:
133	  - Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.). Ensure the message accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (i.e. "add" means a wholly new feature, "update" means an enhancement to an existing feature, "fix" means a bug fix, etc.).
134	  - Do not commit files that likely contain secrets (.env, credentials.json, etc). Warn the user if they specifically request to commit those files
135	  - Draft a concise (1-2 sentences) commit message that focuses on the "why" rather than the "what"
136	  - Ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose
137	3. Run the following commands in parallel:
138	   - Add relevant untracked files to the staging area.
139	   - Create the commit with a message, ending with the attribution lines given in the conversation's system-reminder, when one is present.
140	   - Run git status after the commit completes to verify success.
